  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Differentiating the function f(x)=a cos(x^a) using the chain rule.</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the \(1^{\textbf{st}}, 2^{\textbf{nd}}, 3^{\textbf{rd}},\) and \(4^{\textbf{th}}\) derivatives of the following function:</p>
<ol>
    <p>\(  \displaystyle f(x) = {@fx@} \)</p>
  <li>
    <p>\(  f'(x) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>\(  f''(x) = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>\( f'''(x) = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>\(  f''''(x) = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>
  </li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Let's find the \(1^{\textbf{st}}, 2^{\textbf{nd}}, 3^{\textbf{rd}},\) and \(4^{\textbf{th}}\) derivatives of the given function.</p>

<p>To differentiate the function \( \displaystyle f(x) = {@fx@}\) with respect to \(x\), we can use the chain rule.</p> 
<p>The chain rule states that if you have a function \(g(u)\) and a function \(f(x)\) where \(u\) is a function of \(x\), then the derivative of their composition is given by:</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle
(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)
\)</p>
<p><strong> 1st Derivative </strong>  of \( \displaystyle f(x) = {@fx@}\) let's apply chain rule</p>
<p>In our case, \(\displaystyle g(x) = {@u@}\)</p><p> and \(\displaystyle f(u) ={@cf2@} \cos(u)\), where \( \displaystyle u =  {@u@}\).</p>

<p>Let's find the derivatives step by step:</p>

<ol>
<li>Find the derivative of \(u\) with respect to \(x\):
 <p>  \( \displaystyle
   u =  {@u@} \implies \frac{du}{dx} =  {@diff_u@}
   \)
</p></li>

<li>Find the derivative of \(f(u) ={@cf2@}  \cos(u)\) with respect to \(u\):
 <p>  \( \displaystyle
     f(u) ={@cf2@}  \cos(u) \implies   \frac{df}{du} = -{@cf2@} \sin(u)
   \)<p></li>

<li>Now, apply the chain rule to find the derivative of \(  \displaystyle f(x) ={@fx@}\) with respect to \(x\):
<p>   \( \displaystyle
   \begin{align*}
   \frac{df}{dx} &= \frac{df}{du} \cdot \frac{du}{dx} \\
   &= -{@cf2@} \sin(u) \cdot {@diff_u@} \\
   &= {@ta1@}
   \end{align*}
   \)</p></li>
</ol>

<p>So, the derivative of \( \displaystyle f(x) ={@fx@}\) with respect to \(x\) is:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle 
f'(x) = {@ta1@}
\)<p>

<p>We will still apply chain rule to the rest of the derivatives:</p>
<p><strong>2nd Derivative:</strong></p>
<p>To find the 2nd derivative, differentiate \(f'(x)\):</p>
<p>\(  \displaystyle f''(x) = \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ta1@}\right)\)</p>
<p>Evaluating to get:</p>
<p>\(  \displaystyle f''(x) = {@ta2@}\)</p>

<p> <strong>3rd Derivative:</strong></p>
<p>To find the 3rd derivative, differentiate \(f''(x)\):</p>
<p>\(  \displaystyle f'''(x) = \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ta2@}\right)\)</p>
<p>Evaluating to get:</p>
<p>\(  \displaystyle f'''(x) = {@ta3@}\)</p>

<p><strong>4th Derivative:</strong></p>
<p>To find the 4th derivative, differentiate \(f'''(x)\):</p>
<p>\(  \displaystyle f''''(x) = \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ta3@}\right)\)</p>
<p>Evaluating to get:</p>
<p>\(  \displaystyle f''''(x) = {@ta4@}\)</p>]]></text>
